# answered by claude-opus-5-5, prompt /lib/phil/senses b350a3107fa4, 2026-10-19
common_form	one
more_perfect	one
alike	one
equal	one
# The general principle in S3 ("common form", "participates more perfectly") is applied in S4 to the case of the divine nature. For Father and Son this is numerically one nature, whereas air and fire share heat only as a common quality.
# The text treats this as the general claim applied to a case, not as a change in what would make the claim true. So I read each claim in one sense and found no bridge.
